<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1843 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Dark Eyes</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ru">Очи чёрные</bdi> / <bdi lang="ru-Latn">Ochi chyornye</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Russian love song from 1843, sung to a waltz tune of 1879. It became one of the best-known Russian songs abroad.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Yevhen Hrebinka</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Florian Hermann (melody from his Valse hommage, Op. 21, 1879)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Russian romance (gypsy-style romance)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Russian Empire</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words first published 17 January 1843; melody published 1879</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Yevhen Hrebinka (first published 1843))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Dark Eyes&quot; (in Russian Ochi chyornye, literally &quot;Black Eyes&quot;) is a Russian romance, a sentimental art song. Its words are by the Ukrainian poet and writer Yevhen Hrebinka, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a>, who first published the poem in his own Russian translation in Literaturnaya Gazeta on 17 January 1843. The words appeared in a songbook in 1874, without a melody.</p><p>The tune now sung to them was taken from the &quot;Valse hommage&quot;, Op. 21, for piano, by Florian Hermann, a composer of German-Polish origin working in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, published in 1879. The sheet-music historian James Fuld reports that a Soviet musicologist told him the song was not a Russian traditional song but a cabaret song, published in 1884 and reprinted in 1897 in a songbook by A. Gutheil, where it was called a &quot;Gypsy romance based on the melody of Florian Hermann's Valse Hommage&quot;.</p><p>The singer Feodor Chaliapin made his own version, with changed words and further verses, and dedicated it to his future wife. He sang it with Rachmaninoff at the piano, and Rachmaninoff especially valued one of Chaliapin's recordings of it. Chaliapin's version became a typical example of the gypsy-style Russian romance.</p><p>A second widely played version was written by the Italian-born British composer Adalgiso Ferraris, who spent many years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> before 1915. He published it there in 1910, with the German editor Otto Kuhl, as &quot;Schwarze Augen&quot;, and again on 9 October 1931, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Editions Salabert and Jacques Liber, as &quot;Tes yeux noirs (impression russe)&quot;. It was one of his main successes of the 1920s and 1930s. Albert Sandler played it in 1932, a British Pathé film of 1934 shows Ferraris in the orchestra of Alfredo and his Gypsy band playing it, and Al Bowlly sang it in 1939 as &quot;Black Eyes&quot;, with English words by Albert Mellor.</p><p>The melody has also been claimed elsewhere. In Rebeca Chávez's 2010 documentary, the Cuban musician Sindo Garay (born 1867) said that he had composed it in the 1890s as the bolero &quot;Ojos negros que fascinan&quot;, for a Russian choir-girl of a visiting opera company, and that the musicians took it back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The Sundanese folk song &quot;Panon Hideung&quot; is derived from &quot;Dark Eyes&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A romance addressed to the dark eyes of a beloved, in the sentimental, gypsy-style manner of the Russian romance.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Russian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Russian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Очи черные, очи страстные!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Oči černye, oči strastnye!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Black eyes, passionate eyes!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Очи жгучие и прекрасные!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Oči žgučie i prekrasnye!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Burning eyes, and beautiful!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Как люблю я вас! Как боюсь я вас!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Kak ljublju ja vas! Kak bojusʹ ja vas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How I love you! How I fear you!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Знать увидел вас я в недобрый час!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Znatʹ uvidel vas ja v nedobryj čas!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I must have seen you in an evil hour!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Ох, недаром вы глубины темней!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Ox, nedarom vy glubiny temnej!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh, not for nothing are you darker than the deep!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Вижу траур в вас по душе моей,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Vižu traur v vas po duše moej,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I see in you mourning for my soul,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Вижу пламя в вас я победное:</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Vižu plamja v vas ja pobednoe:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I see in you a victorious flame:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Сожжено на нем сердце бедное.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Sožženo na nem serdce bednoe.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On it a poor heart is burnt.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Но не грустен я, не печален я,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">No ne grusten ja, ne pečalen ja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But I am not sad, I am not sorrowful,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Утешительна мне судьба моя:</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Utešitelʹna mne sudʹba moja:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My fate is a comfort to me:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Всё, что лучшего в жизни Бог дал нам,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Vsë, čto lučšego v žizni Bog dal nam,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All the best in life that God gave us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">В жертву отдал я огневым глазам!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">V žertvu otdal ja ognevym glazam!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have given as a sacrifice to fiery eyes!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Yevhen Hrebinka (1843). Text from <a href="https://ru.wikisource.org/wiki/%D0%A7%D1%91%D1%80%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%B8_(%D0%93%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B1%D1%91%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B0)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.4: 'Знать' is colloquial for 'it seems, evidently': 'I must have seen you', not 'to know'.</li><li>line 1.4: 'в недобрый час', literally 'in an unkind hour': an ill-omened moment.</li><li>line 1.1: 'черные' is literally 'black' (the song is known in English as 'Dark Eyes').</li><li>line 2.1: 'глубины темней', darker than the depth(s) (of the sea is implied, not said).</li><li>line 3.3: the original says 'gave us' (нам) and 'I gave' (я): kept as written.</li><li>Text: as given by Wikisource for Hrebinka's 1843 poem; the sung version adds and changes stanzas.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="jA9hhemVXI8" data-title="Dark Eyes: Feodor Chaliapin's recording (Paris, 1927)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Feodor Chaliapin's recording (Paris, 1927)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel TheMrMelomann. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jA9hhemVXI8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Chaliapin's version (Feodor Chaliapin)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new and changed words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Schwarze Augen (Adalgiso Ferraris, with Otto Kuhl)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1910</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tes yeux noirs (impression russe) (Adalgiso Ferraris, with Jacques Liber)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1931</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Black Eyes (Al Bowlly, words by Albert Mellor)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Panon Hideung (Sundanese folk song)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">derived from</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Valse hommage, Op. 21 (Florian Hermann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1879</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody borrowed</span></li></ul></section>
